I wonder if BoneAttachment3D should listen for explicit changes to its local transform (using the notification) and basically directly update skeleton at whatever point in the frame it happens.

So basically setting `BoneAttachment.position = foo` does exactly the same thing as
```
Skeleton3D.set_bone_pose_position(bone_idx, Skeleton3D.get_bone_global_pose(Skeleton3D.get_bone_parent(bone_idx)).affine_inverse() * foo)
```

Well this is quite inefficient since this invalidates the cache, but theoretically speaking it would be really nice to have an implementation of `get_bone_global_pose` which does not use the global pose cache, and walks up the tree, since that is usually much less operations than updating the entire tree.

so something like
```cpp
void BoneAttachment3D::_notification(p_not) {
  switch(p_not) {
  case NOTIFICATION_TRANSFORM_CHANGED:
    Skeleton3D.set_bone_pose_position(bone_idx, Skeleton3D.get_bone_global_pose_uncached(Skeleton3D.get_bone_parent(bone_idx)).affine_inverse() * foo);
  break;
  }
}
```
and so on for rotation and scale

Then, the BoneAttachment3D is completely passive and can work bidirectionally since it's just essentially forwarding function calls. (it needs infinite loop prevention for skeleton updating the attachment, but this is fairly easy to code)

what do you think?

